  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 246 AM CDT Wed Jul 22 2026\n\nAs of 245 AM Wednesday morning, a highly amplified midlevel \npattern remains across the CONUS with ridging across the western\nhalf and troughing across the east. The midlevel ridge axis \nextends from northern CO through southwest KS and into central \nOK. This places the forecast area on the northeastern periphery \nof the ridge axis. Scattered showers and thunderstorms have \ndeveloped across western and northern NE within a weak 700 mb \nWAA pattern. This WAA pattern may expand across portions of \nnorth-central KS after sunrise. Unfortunately, increasing 700 \nmb temperatures (12 to 14 C) across north-central and central KS\nmay preclude thunderstorm development but a small chance exists\nnonetheless. Thanks to the frontal passage Tuesday, more- \nseasonable temperatures are in-store this afternoon with highs \ntopping out in the upper 80s to lower 90s. \n\nThe potential for nocturnal convection increases late Wednesday night \ninto Thursday morning as a strong low and midlevel WAA pattern \nemerges from western NE through portions of central and eastern\nKS. The prospects for any severe weather appear low with\ninstability generally less than 1000 J/kg and effective shear\nless than 30 kt. High PW values around 2\" will support heavy \nrainfall. Scattered showers and thunderstorms may persist \nthrough much of the day Thursday, especially across northeast KS\nwhere weak WAA will continue. Another complex of thunderstorm \nactivity is appearing more and more likely late Thursday into \nFriday. A shortwave trough is forecast to eject into the central\nPlains Thursday evening into Thursday night. Scattered \nthunderstorm development is expected along an inverted trough \naxis across eastern CO/western KS/western NE. As the evening \nprogresses, convection is likely to grow upscale into an MCS and\ntranslate eastward within a 35-45 kt LLJ. The greatest storm \nchances will remain across portions of central and northeast KS.\nAdditional storms are possible Friday afternoon into Saturday \nmorning as the surface trough slides across southeast KS. The \ncore of the midlevel ridge appears to slide back over the \ncentral Plains by next weekend. This will likely yield warmer \ntemperatures, heat indices above 100 degrees, and minimal rain \nchances from Saturday afternoon into next week.",
